  "narration": [
    {
      "at": 0,
      "text": "The model starts with a linear score, z = w·x + b."
    },
    {
      "at": 1.2,
      "text": "The sigmoid turns that score into a probability that is always between 0 and 1."
    },
    {
      "at": 2.8,
      "text": "The curve crosses p = 0.5 where the score is zero; that feature value is the decision boundary."
    },
    {
      "at": 4.8,
      "text": "Changing the weight changes the steepness and can move the boundary, but it does not establish a causal effect."
    },
    {
      "at": 6.4,
      "text": "At the orange point, the height of the curve is the predicted probability for that feature value."
    }
  ],
  "fallback": {
    "text": "Logistic regression first computes a linear score, z = w*x + b, which can be any real number. It then applies the sigmoid function, p = 1 / (1 + exp(-z)), so the predicted probability is always between 0 and 1. The decision boundary is the set of feature values where z = 0, which gives p = 0.5. Increasing the weight makes the S-curve steeper and moves the boundary; changing the bias shifts it. The model describes association in the data, not a causal effect."
  },
  "accessibility": {
    "alt": "A one-dimensional logistic plot shows feature values on the horizontal axis and predicted probability as an S-shaped curve between 0 and 1; a vertical decision boundary sits where probability is 0.5 and shifts as weight, bias, or feature change.",
    "reducedMotion": "static",
    "keyboard": true
  }
